WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.257
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level C one.

2
00:00:05.557 --> 00:00:08.979
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.279 --> 00:00:16.215
Unit thirty-two: Complex Noun Phrases. Today you will pack information into noun phrases for academic writing.

4
00:00:16.515 --> 00:00:19.121
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.067 --> 00:00:23.625
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.483 --> 00:00:25.577
Premodifier.

7
00:00:26.583 --> 00:00:29.256
Premodifier means a word before the head noun.

8
00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:31.141
A new report.

9
00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:34.333
Your turn. Say: Premodifier.

10
00:00:37.167 --> 00:00:38.226
Premodifier.

11
00:00:38.983 --> 00:00:40.263
Postmodifier.

12
00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:44.503
Postmodifier means a word or phrase after the head noun.

13
00:00:45.117 --> 00:00:46.745
A report on climate.

14
00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:50.293
Your turn. Say: Postmodifier.

15
00:00:53.133 --> 00:00:54.343
Postmodifier.

16
00:00:55.083 --> 00:00:55.968
Head noun.

17
00:00:56.983 --> 00:00:59.679
Head noun means the main noun in a noun phrase.

18
00:01:00.283 --> 00:01:01.981
Report is the head noun.

19
00:01:02.583 --> 00:01:05.317
Your turn. Say: Head noun.

20
00:01:08.167 --> 00:01:09.121
Head noun.

21
00:01:09.883 --> 00:01:11.244
Noun cluster.

22
00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:14.795
Noun cluster means a group of nouns together.

23
00:01:15.383 --> 00:01:17.046
A climate change report.

24
00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:20.848
Your turn. Say: Noun cluster.

25
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:24.817
Noun cluster.

26
00:01:25.583 --> 00:01:27.525
Prepositional postmodifier.

27
00:01:28.517 --> 00:01:32.490
Prepositional postmodifier means a phrase with a preposition after the noun.

28
00:01:33.083 --> 00:01:34.688
A report on climate.

29
00:01:35.283 --> 00:01:39.097
Your turn. Say: Prepositional postmodifier.

30
00:01:41.933 --> 00:01:43.852
Prepositional postmodifier.

31
00:01:44.617 --> 00:01:46.465
Relative postmodifier.

32
00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:50.843
Relative postmodifier means a relative clause after the noun.

33
00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:53.194
A report that was published.

34
00:01:53.783 --> 00:01:57.423
Your turn. Say: Relative postmodifier.

35
00:02:00.267 --> 00:02:02.359
Relative postmodifier.

36
00:02:03.117 --> 00:02:04.710
Information density.

37
00:02:05.717 --> 00:02:09.260
Information density means a lot of information in few words.

38
00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:11.687
High information density.

39
00:02:12.283 --> 00:02:15.679
Your turn. Say: Information density.

40
00:02:18.533 --> 00:02:20.394
Information density.

41
00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:22.615
Academic style.

42
00:02:23.617 --> 00:02:26.754
Academic style means the style of research writing.

43
00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:29.071
An academic style.

44
00:02:29.683 --> 00:02:32.754
Your turn. Say: Academic style.

45
00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:37.019
Academic style.

46
00:02:37.833 --> 00:02:41.786
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:46.832
Visal, how can I expand a report into a complex noun phrase?

48
00:02:47.917 --> 00:03:01.134
A comprehensive new report on climate change published last year. Report is the head noun, comprehensive new is the premodifier, and the rest is postmodifier.

49
00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:04.505
What kinds of postmodifier are there?

50
00:03:05.617 --> 00:03:19.722
Prepositional phrases: a report on climate. Relative clauses: a report that I read. Participles: a report published last year. To-infinitives: a plan to reduce waste.

51
00:03:20.817 --> 00:03:21.969
Why use them?

52
00:03:23.083 --> 00:03:36.094
They increase information density, which suits academic style. Spoken English uses short clauses, while writing packs the same information into noun phrases. Do not overload them.

53
00:03:37.233 --> 00:03:38.861
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:39.717 --> 00:03:45.594
Grammar focus: Noun phrase: premodifier plus head noun plus postmodifier.

55
00:03:45.894 --> 00:03:47.139
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:50.744
A comprehensive new report on climate change.

57
00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:53.882
The recent proposal to reduce waste.

58
00:03:54.783 --> 00:03:57.305
An influential study published last year.

59
00:03:58.267 --> 00:04:05.537
Build noun phrases with premodifiers before the head noun and postmodifiers (prepositional, relative, participial) after it.

60
00:04:06.283 --> 00:04:09.714
Be careful! Don't say: A report new on climate.

61
00:04:11.667 --> 00:04:14.142
Say: A new report on climate.

62
00:04:14.442 --> 00:04:16.268
Put the premodifier first.

63
00:04:17.067 --> 00:04:19.498
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:21.870
Packing In Information.

65
00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:25.899
A comprehensive new report on climate change was published last week.

66
00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:30.513
The proposal to reduce plastic waste received wide support.

67
00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:34.800
Researchers analysed data collected over twenty years.

68
00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:38.577
These noun phrases carry a lot of information.

69
00:04:39.567 --> 00:04:43.633
Academic texts rely on them for a dense, precise style.

70
00:04:44.733 --> 00:04:48.781
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:49.617 --> 00:04:52.800
In 'a new report on climate', the head noun is blank.

72
00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:56.487
A, new. B, report. C, climate.

73
00:05:00.033 --> 00:05:01.859
The answer is B: report.

74
00:05:03.017 --> 00:05:05.945
'On climate change' is a blank postmodifier.

75
00:05:06.245 --> 00:05:09.956
A, prepositional. B, relative. C, negative.

76
00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:16.138
The answer is A: prepositional.

77
00:05:17.283 --> 00:05:20.130
'Published last year' is a blank postmodifier.

78
00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:24.943
A, participial. B, finite. C, negative.

79
00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:30.743
The answer is A: participial.

80
00:05:31.883 --> 00:05:34.220
Complex noun phrases increase blank.

81
00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:38.405
A, information density. B, spelling. C, volume.

82
00:05:41.967 --> 00:05:44.779
The answer is A: information density.

83
00:05:45.917 --> 00:05:49.391
According to the reading, how long was the data collected?

84
00:05:49.691 --> 00:05:54.226
A, twenty years. B, two years. C, ten days.

85
00:05:57.767 --> 00:05:59.662
The answer is A: twenty years.

86
00:06:00.867 --> 00:06:04.367
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:05.217 --> 00:06:10.316
Expand 'a report' into a complex noun phrase with a premodifier and a postmodifier.

88
00:06:19.117 --> 00:06:20.629
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:20.929 --> 00:06:34.927
A detailed annual report on the school's progress was published today. Detailed annual is the premodifier, and on the school's progress is the postmodifier. Report is the head noun.

90
00:06:35.227 --> 00:06:36.889
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:37.783 --> 00:06:45.045
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and noun phrase: premodifier plus head noun plus postmodifier.

92
00:06:45.345 --> 00:06:50.354
Today's value is logical rigor. Follow your reasoning wherever it honestly leads.

93
00:06:50.654 --> 00:06:54.038
See you in Unit thirty-three. Goodbye!

94
00:06:54.338 --> 00:06:57.547
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

